WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.410
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:10.410
This piece of work is about reducing the time
needed to simulate elastic deformations by

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.059
means of precomputation.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:15.870
Okay, so what does the term precomputation
mean?

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:21.369
If we are at an open book exam and we are
short on time, which is, basically, every

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:27.490
time, it would be much better to do a precomputation
step, namely studying at home for a few days

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:32.720
before, and then, when we are there, we are
endowed with quite a bit of knowledge, and

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.840
are guaranteed to do much better than trying
to grasp the simplest concepts on the spot.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.820
This precomputation step we only have to do
once, and it almost doesn't matter how lengthy

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.790
it is, because after that, we can answer any
question in this topic in the future.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:55.000
Well, sometimes passing exams is not as easy
as described here, but a fair bit of precomputation

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.810
often goes a long way.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:57.810
Just saying.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:03.480
The authors have identified 3 major bottlenecks
in already existing precomputation techniques,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.530
and proposed optimizations to speed them up
considerably at the cost of higher memory

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:09.530
consumption.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:15.170
For instance, the algorithm is trained on
a relatively large set of training pose examples.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.170
If we don't have enough of these training
examples, the quality of the animations will

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:24.140
be unsatisfactory, but if we use too many,
that's too resource intensive.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:28.500
We have to choose just the right amount, and
the right kinds of poses, which is a highly

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.329
non-trivial process.

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:34.640
Note that this training is not the same kind
of training we are used to see with neural

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.640
networks.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.770
This work doesn't have anything to do with
neural networks at all!

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.369
The results of the new technique are clearly
very close to the results we would obtain

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:49.460
with standard methods, however, the computation
time is 20 to 2000 times less.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.179
In the more favorable cases, computing deformations
that would take several hours can take less

00:01:55.179 --> 00:01:56.740
than a second.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.920
That is one jaw-dropping result and a hefty
value proposition indeed.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.740
This example shows that after a short precomputation
step, we can start torturing this poor armadillo

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.180
and expect high-quality elastic deformations.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.430
And there is a lot of other things to be learned
from the paper.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:18.910
Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization, augmented
Krylov iterations, Newton-PCG solvers.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:23.670
Essentially, if you pick up a dry textbook
on linear algebra, and for every technique

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.430
you see there, you ask what on Earth this
is useful for, you wouldn't have to go through

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:34.630
hundreds of works, you would find a ton of
answers in just this one absolutely amazing

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:35.630
paper.

